;;; THE GAPS: what the notes say that the roads or the series layer state
;;; differently (read this first).
;;;
;;; (G1) THE INTEGRAND IS A FUNCTION ON A SET u, NOT "phi on the trace".  phi is a
;;;      member of FUN(u, CC), continuous on the subspace u, with TRACE(gamma) inside
;;;      u.  The notes' case is u = TRACE(gamma, a, b) itself (a set: trace-is-set).
;;;      CA-1's integral formula will integrate f(w)/(w - z) as a VNB-LAMBDA over
;;;      SOME domain; the statements here integrate VNB-LAMBDA(w, u, phi(w) *
;;;      recip(w - z)) with u a free parameter, so the composition is one citation
;;;      exactly when CA-1's integrand is that term with u its domain.  If it is
;;;      spelled with `/' or over another domain, a bridge is needed, and the tree
;;;      had NO congruence law for LINE-INT IN THE INTEGRAND (two integrands equal
;;;      on the trace have equal integrals; line-int-interior-congruence moves the
;;;      PATH).  CA-1's theorem-library/winding-number-laws.scm (written the same
;;;      day) proves it as `line-int-domain-congruence'; with it the bridge from
;;;      another spelling of the kernel is one citation plus the pointwise
;;;      equation.
;;; (G2) THE DISC.  The notes' "B(xi, R) disjoint from the trace, |u - xi| <= r < R"
;;;      is written as: every w of u has r <= |w - c|, and |z - c| < r.  The
;;;      circle |w - c| = r of 3.7 is the special case; any set at distance >= r
;;;      from c will do.  Lemma 3.33's conclusion "analytic on the complement of the
;;;      trace" needs, for each xi off the trace, a positive r with the trace at
;;;      distance >= r from xi: a distance-to-a-compact-set lemma, not proven here.
;;; (G3) THE COEFFICIENTS are written  a_k = int_gamma phi(w) * recip(w - c)^(k+1) dw
;;;      (a POWER OF THE RECIPROCAL, exponent succ(k)), not phi(w)/(w - c)^(k+1).
;;;      The two agree by cc-recip-mul and induction; that bridge is not proven
;;;      here because nothing here needs it.
;;; (G4) "f IS THE SUM OF THE SERIES" is CC-SERIES-CONVERGES-TO of the ordered
;;;      partial sums (the notes' meaning).  The tree's analyticity predicate
